He bought all things in all places, all things for gaining a livelihood. He was very diligent, careful, and vigilant. He sought and dedicated himself not to only one thing. He never tired; he was not lazy nor listless. Indeed, he attended to his affairs day and night. Not vain nor futile was his work. He labored industriously to gain sustenance, his daily bread, his trail rations, his livelihood, his maintenance, the source of life, the means of living, and provisions for traveling. He looked to the future and saw before him that sometimes poverty and misery would develop for and strike him. He would meet it.

He saved up wealth to protect and divide justly among his children.

Everything frightened him, lest sometime something be brought upon him: gossip, calumny, slander, perjury, covetous deeds, greedy dealings. Perhaps they might thus snare or snatch something of his goods and property. They might covet or seek after it. Or in something justice might be demanded; there would be accusations, and it would be made public. Then he might have to pay;
